WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
THE STATE OF LINUX:
Did you know that Microsoft actually uses linux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:09.960
to run much of its cloud infrastructure? And it’s&nbsp;
not like they’re forced to either. Before 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.760
Microsoft used to build watered down versions&nbsp;
of Windows for running networks, databases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.820
and other cloud infrastructure. So, the switch&nbsp;
to Linux was very much a voluntary one. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.900
it’s surprising that Microsoft was willing&nbsp;
to put aside their ego and switch to Linux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.600
objectively speaking, this switch was not&nbsp;
too surprising given that Linux is usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:36.960
the preferred OS for backend infrastructure. It’s&nbsp;
simply more stable, reliable, secure, flexible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.900
and much cheaper to operate and maintain.&nbsp;
Hearing this, I don’t think you’d be surprised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:48.840
to hear that Microsoft isn’t the only one that&nbsp;
uses Linux. Amazon, Dell, Facebook, Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.840
and even NASA use Linux for various portions&nbsp;
of their business. Yet, despite it’s popularity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:00.360
amongst these institutions, Linux is virtually&nbsp;
nonexistent amongst everyday consumers. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.300
technically, Android is built on Linux and Android&nbsp;
is the most popular operating system in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:13.140
so you could make that argument for Linux, but&nbsp;
I’m talking about the consumer PC market. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:21.240
74% use Windows, 14% use MacOS, and only 5%&nbsp;
use Linux. This isn’t to say that Linux isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.220
good for desktop use though. For decades,&nbsp;
hardcore users like programmers, engineers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.380
and tech enthusiasts have not only been using&nbsp;
Linux themselves, but they’ve been preaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:38.280
about why it’s way better than Windows and Mac.&nbsp;
Yet, no one else seems to care. So, if Linux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:44.340
truly is the objectively better OS and if even&nbsp;
Microsoft uses Linux over Windows, why don’t we?

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.600
USELESSLY FREE:&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.920
One of the top advantages that many Linux&nbsp;
enthusiasts cite is the fact that Linux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.920
is free. They argue that instead of paying $100&nbsp;
for a Windows license, you can pay no money for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:05.280
Linux. This way, you’d not only be saving money,&nbsp;
but you’d also be getting a superior OS. From&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.860
their perspective, this scenario is clearly a win&nbsp;
win, but from the average consumer's perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:16.980
it’s not quite that simple. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;
Linux being free doesn’t mean much to the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:23.580
consumer because to them, Windows and MacOS are&nbsp;
also free. It’s not like they have to go out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:29.700
buy a license for either of these. They just go&nbsp;
out and buy the computer and the OS comes along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:35.220
with the purchase. So, not only would the average&nbsp;
consumer not save money by switching to Linux, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.980
they’d also feel that they’re not using something&nbsp;
that they paid for. Really, the only people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:47.100
can save money by using Linux is people who build&nbsp;
their own PCs. And PC builders are by far the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:53.100
minority within the PC market. Not to mention,&nbsp;
a substantial portion of PC builders are gamers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.680
and Windows is simply the best choice for gaming&nbsp;
because that’s what games are built for. And yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:04.140
there are ways that you can run Windows games on&nbsp;
Linux, but this kind of defeats the entire purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:10.020
of Linux. One of the biggest advantages of Linux&nbsp;
is that it can run many things better natively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.360
but games are not usually one of those things&nbsp;
because they’re specifically coded to run on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.220
Windows. Also, if someone is willing to spend&nbsp;
a thousand dollars or thousands of dollars on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:25.320
a gaming PC, I highly doubt that the cost of a&nbsp;
windows license is a serious consideration. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:30.360
if it was, if they’re savvy enough to build a PC,&nbsp;
they’re probably savvy enough to migrate windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:36.000
from an old computer or just pirate it. And that&nbsp;
brings us to our next point which is that getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.360
windows for free is extraordinarily easy. While&nbsp;
Windows is not free on paper, Microsoft has gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.220
out of their way to make sure that you can get it&nbsp;
for free if you want to. You don’t even need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:52.140
pirate it. You can literally download it straight&nbsp;
from Microsoft’s website and install onto your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:57.780
new computer using a USB stick. And there’s&nbsp;
literally no consequences for not activating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.780
Windows. You get 30 days to activate, but even if&nbsp;
you don’t, the only thing you lose is your ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:10.260
to personalize. In other words, you don’t lose&nbsp;
any functionality whatsoever. So, for end users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.300
whether you’re buying a prebuilt or a building&nbsp;
your own PC, Linux offers virtually no financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:20.280
benefit. Someone who does financially benefit&nbsp;
from choosing Linux, however, is institutions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.560
Schools, universities, companies, and computer&nbsp;
manufacturers all have to pay for Windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:31.680
licenses. Now, I’m sure they don’t pay anywhere&nbsp;
near as much as $100 per license given that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.080
probably get some sort of bulk order discount.&nbsp;
But, even if it’s $30 or $40 per license, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.220
these institutions are ordering tens of thousands&nbsp;
or even hundreds of thousands of computers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:47.160
Windows does end up costing them several hundred&nbsp;
thousand dollars if not millions. So, switching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.900
to Linux could be a smart financial decision,&nbsp;
but this option quickly becomes impossible when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.720
you realize that none of your students or&nbsp;
employees actually know how to use Linux.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.500
THE COMMAND LINE:&nbsp;

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:07.260
One of the biggest obstacles for Linux adoption&nbsp;
is that it’s just not that easy to use. Linux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.940
user interfaces have made strides within the&nbsp;
past several years especially Ubuntu. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.680
some level of familiarity with the command line&nbsp;
is still extremely helpful when it comes to using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:22.200
Linux. To many tech enthusiasts, this is what&nbsp;
makes Linux so great. Instead of having to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.340
with generic user interfaces that are built for&nbsp;
general consumption, you can use the command line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:31.020
to do very specific tasks. While this is great for&nbsp;
people who are comfortable with the command line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:36.840
this just seems completely unnecessary to people&nbsp;
who aren’t. The customizability and flexibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:42.060
that the command line unlocks are simply not&nbsp;
applicable to the average person. Virtually all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.980
these people are primarily doing extremely generic&nbsp;
tasks like working on word docs and excel sheets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:51.960
or replying to emails. And the freedom that the&nbsp;
command line offers doesn’t seem like a freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:57.600
at all. To them, the command line simply comes off&nbsp;
as an unnecessarily complicated way to accomplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.160
extremely straight forward tasks. Even for the&nbsp;
people who could benefit from the command line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.660
more time than not, they don’t want to spend time&nbsp;
learning about it because it seems super daunting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:11.880
They often feel like they need to know coding to&nbsp;
use the command line, and they often fear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.500
they’re one wrong command away from corrupting&nbsp;
something and destroying their computer. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.300
is especially true when you’re running a command&nbsp;
using administrative authority. It just feels like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.680
you’re messing with something that you shouldn’t&nbsp;
be messing with. Aside from having to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.600
the command line, with Linux, you also have to&nbsp;
deal with installing everything manually. Once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:36.120
again, this is actually one of the advantages of&nbsp;
Linux. Linux comes with virtually no bloatware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:41.460
so you can install only what you need. This is&nbsp;
one of the reasons why Linux machines with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:47.160
same specs tend to run faster and smoother than&nbsp;
windows machines. But, the average user doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:52.740
always view this situation in the same light. For&nbsp;
them, all this means is that Linux does not work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:58.800
out of the box, and that’s not an advantage,&nbsp;
that’s a disadvantage. Many of them actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:04.020
like much of the software that Linux users would&nbsp;
classify as bloatware. Take Mac OS for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:10.620
Macs generally come preinstalled with Apple TV,&nbsp;
Apple Music, iCloud, Facetime, and iMessage. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:15.000
a power user who’s only looking to use their Mac&nbsp;
for productive purposes like coding, software like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.640
Apple TV and iCloud may seem completely useless.&nbsp;
They’re probably never going to open Apple TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:27.060
on this machine and they far prefer GitHub over&nbsp;
iCloud. But, for the everyday user, they may love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:32.760
that their photos are automatically synced through&nbsp;
iCloud and that they can text through iMesage. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.680
them, all of this preinstalled software isn’t&nbsp;
bloware, it’s what makes the computer functional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:44.700
out of the box. As Apple likes to call it “It&nbsp;
just works”. Both Windows and MacOS just work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:51.180
Linux on the other hand doesn’t. And even though&nbsp;
that’s what makes Linux so flexible, customizable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:56.760
and free, most people simply don’t care enough&nbsp;
about that to learn the command line or a new OS.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.900
VIRTUALLY NO NEED:
While many Linux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:08.580
users will swear by Linux, the bottomline is that&nbsp;
the average person simply has no need of it. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:13.560
fact, even power users don’t really need it simply&nbsp;
because MacOS and Windows have actually gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:19.020
pretty good at emulating the best features of&nbsp;
Linux. Take coding for example. One of the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:24.780
reasons that developers love Linux is because it’s&nbsp;
based on Unix. Unix is an OS that’s specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:30.960
designed with programmers in mind. Windows is not&nbsp;
based on Unix, so it often makes sense to choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:38.220
Linux over Windows. But, something that is based&nbsp;
on Unix is MacOS. So, not only does Mac offer many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:43.740
of the same coding advantages as Linux, but it’s&nbsp;
also more user friendly and polished. This is why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:50.040
MacOS is extremely popular amongst developers.&nbsp;
Globally, the Mac only has 8.9% marketshare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:55.980
but amongst developers, it has 44% marketshare.&nbsp;
Aside from most people not having any need for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:00.960
Linux, they also find that Linux actively&nbsp;
makes them less productive. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.480
not all Linux distros come with wifi drivers which&nbsp;
means that you’ll have to manually install drivers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.280
just to connect to the internet. But, atleast&nbsp;
that’s only a one time issue. Something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.260
you’ll have to constantly deal with is lack of&nbsp;
support. Even something as rudimentary and basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:21.780
as Microsoft Office is not natively supported on&nbsp;
Linux. So, you’ll have to jump through a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:27.000
loopholes just to get access to basic software.&nbsp;
This same rule also applies to software like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:32.100
Photoshop, Premiere Pro, Autodesk Inventor, and&nbsp;
basically anything else that doesn’t directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:37.260
involve coding. But atleast, you can make those&nbsp;
software work by jumping through a few hoops.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:42.600
There are several websites and software that&nbsp;
simply will not work on Linux whatsoever. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.700
example, nothing to do with Pearson Education&nbsp;
works. If you’re a student and have a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:53.520
of classes that use Pearson, this makes Linux&nbsp;
completely unviable. So, that’s pretty much all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:59.100
of the objective reasons that the PC community at&nbsp;
large hasn’t adopted Linux. Linux’s free nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:05.820
isn’t all that helpful, the command line and UI is&nbsp;
rather daunting, and Linux often poses a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:11.100
challenges than solutions for most people. But,&nbsp;
there’s a subjective side to all of this as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:15.840
and I don’t think this video would be complete&nbsp;
if we didn’t discuss that as well. The truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:20.580
is that Linux comes along with a negative&nbsp;
social stigma. Usually, Linux is associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:25.860
with nerds and broke people. In terms of social&nbsp;
prestige, Mac would probably be first place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:31.140
Windows would be second place, and Linux would be&nbsp;
last place. So, why would the average person want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:35.520
to go out of their way to downgrade themselves&nbsp;
socially when they can just get a mac or windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:41.700
machine. The answer is that there is no reason and&nbsp;
that’s why Linux adoption is where its at today.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:48.060
THE TRUTH ABOUT LINUX:
In the end, while Linux may be superior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:53.640
in certain use cases, almost all of these use&nbsp;
cases are professional. They involve databases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.840
networks, and cloud computing which are all&nbsp;
things the average person will never deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:03.000
from the backend. Also, even amongst the people&nbsp;
who do use Linux for professional purposes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.460
I bet most of them have a Windows or Mac machine&nbsp;
at home for personal use cases. While there will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.940
be some enthusiasts that will argue that&nbsp;
Linux is better for personal use as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:18.360
this is simply not true. And honestly, I think a&nbsp;
lot of these guys have an ulterior motive. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:23.640
of them use Linux not because it’s superior but&nbsp;
because of ideological reasons. They’re usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.440
anti big tech and think that open source projects&nbsp;
like Linux should be supported and developed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:34.200
much as possible. And this is usually the root&nbsp;
reason that they’re so pro Linux, but as for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:40.560
rest of us, well, we usually just don’t care. As&nbsp;
Murray Bauman once said “People don't spend their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.600
lives trying to get a look at what's behind the&nbsp;
curtain. They like the curtain. It provides them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:51.960
stability, comfort, definition.” And within the&nbsp;
PC market, MacOS and Windows are that curtain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

